{
  id: "u3-codes", unit: "u3", title: "Standard Digital Codes: BCD, Gray Code and Unicode", minutes: 10,
  summary: "Why devices need agreed binary codes, and how BCD, Gray code and Unicode (UTF-8) represent numbers and text.",
  source: "Unit III slides 5–6",
  intro: "<p>A computer only stores 0s and 1s. To exchange the letter “A”, the digit “7” or the symbol “₹”, sender and receiver must agree on <b>which bit pattern means what</b>. Those agreements are <b>standard digital codes</b>.</p>",
  definition: "Standard digital codes are sets of rules that assign a unique binary code (a sequence of 0s and 1s) to characters, numbers, symbols and control commands, so that computers, communication devices and networking equipment can process and transmit information.",
  why: "<p>Without a common code, a correctly delivered message would still be unreadable — the protocol problem from Unit I. Codes also serve special purposes: BCD keeps decimal digits exact, Gray code minimises errors in sensors, and Unicode covers every language.</p>",
  how: "<div class='table-wrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Full form</th><th>Bit size</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody>" +
    "<tr><td><b>BCD</b></td><td>Binary-Coded Decimal</td><td>4 or 8</td><td>Encoding decimal digits (0–9)</td></tr>" +
    "<tr><td><b>ASCII</b></td><td>American Standard Code for Information Interchange</td><td>7 (or 8)</td><td>Text in computers and devices</td></tr>" +
    "<tr><td><b>EBCDIC</b></td><td>Extended Binary Coded Decimal Interchange Code</td><td>8</td><td>IBM mainframes and legacy systems</td></tr>" +
    "<tr><td><b>Unicode</b></td><td>Universal Character Encoding Standard</td><td>8–32</td><td>Worldwide, multilingual text</td></tr>" +
    "<tr><td><b>Gray code</b></td><td>Binary Reflected Gray Code</td><td>Varies</td><td>Error minimisation in digital electronics</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" +
    "<h3>BCD (Binary-Coded Decimal)</h3><p>Each <b>decimal digit</b> is written separately as a 4-bit binary number (8421 weights). 59 becomes <code>0101 1001</code> in BCD, whereas pure binary gives <code>111011</code>.</p>" +
    "<h3>Gray code</h3><p>Consecutive values differ in <b>only one bit</b>. If a sensor is read at the instant a value changes, at most one bit can be uncertain, so the reading is never wildly wrong.</p>" +
    "<h3>Unicode</h3><p>One code number (a <i>code point</i>) for every character in every script — English, Hindi, Marathi, emoji and more — stored as 8 to 32 bits per character (UTF-8, UTF-16 or UTF-32).</p>",
  deep: "<div class='codebox'>Binary → Gray:   g = b XOR (b >> 1)        e.g. 5 = 101 → 101 XOR 010 = 111\nGray → Binary:   b = g XOR (g>>1) XOR (g>>2) ...   (running XOR from the MSB)\n\nDecimal  Binary  Gray        Decimal  Binary  Gray\n   0      000    000            4      100    110\n   1      001    001            5      101    111\n   2      010    011            6      110    101\n   3      011    010            7      111    100</div>" +
    "<p><b>BCD details.</b> Only 0000–1001 are valid; 1010–1111 are unused, so BCD wastes 6 of 16 combinations. <i>Packed BCD</i> stores two digits per byte. BCD avoids decimal-to-binary rounding errors, which is why calculators, digital clocks and financial systems use it.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Unicode encodings.</b> Code points run from U+0000 to U+10FFFF. <b>UTF-8</b> uses 1–4 bytes and is backward-compatible with ASCII (U+0000–U+007F are single bytes identical to ASCII). Examples: “A” U+0041 → <code>41</code>; “₹” U+20B9 → <code>E2 82 B9</code>; “क” U+0915 → <code>E0 A4 95</code>. <b>UTF-16</b> uses 2 or 4 bytes, and <b>UTF-32</b> always uses 4.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Gray code in Unit IV.</b> QPSK and QAM constellations label neighbouring points with Gray code, so the most likely symbol error (to an adjacent point) causes only a <i>single</i> bit error.</p>",
  examples: ["BCD: seven-segment displays on calculators and digital clocks", "Gray code: rotary shaft encoders in robots and machines", "Unicode: Hindi text in WhatsApp, emoji in messages", "ASCII/EBCDIC: text files on PCs and on IBM mainframes"],
  terms: [["Code point", "Unicode's number for a character, written U+XXXX."], ["UTF-8", "Variable-length (1–4 byte) Unicode encoding, ASCII-compatible."], ["8421 code", "The weights of BCD bits."], ["Gray code", "Code where successive values differ by one bit."], ["Packed BCD", "Two BCD digits stored in one byte."]],
  exam: ["Standard digital codes assign unique binary patterns to characters, numbers, symbols and control commands.", "Learn the table: BCD (4/8 bits), ASCII (7/8), EBCDIC (8), Unicode (8–32), Gray (varies).", "BCD encodes each decimal digit separately in 4 bits.", "Gray code: one bit changes between successive values (error minimisation).", "Unicode: worldwide multilingual text."],
  recap: "BCD = one nibble per decimal digit. Gray = one bit changes at a time. ASCII/EBCDIC = text (7/8 bits). Unicode = every language (UTF-8, 1–4 bytes).",
  viz: { id: "v-codes", intro: "One decimal number is shown in binary, BCD and Gray code, counting up step by step." },
  sim: { id: "s-codes", tasks: ["Enter 59 and compare its binary and BCD forms. Which uses more bits?", "Count through Gray code from 0 to 15 and confirm only one bit changes each step.", "Type “Hi ₹” and see how many UTF-8 bytes each character needs."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "BCD stands for:", o: ["Binary Character Data", "Binary-Coded Decimal", "Basic Code Digit", "Bit Coded Data"], a: 1, e: "Binary-Coded Decimal encodes each decimal digit in binary.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Which code is used for worldwide multilingual text?", o: ["BCD", "Gray", "Unicode", "EBCDIC"], a: 2, e: "Unicode covers all the world's scripts.", d: 1 },
    { q: "The BCD representation of decimal 47 is:", o: ["0010 1111", "0100 0111", "0101 0011", "0111 0100"], a: 1, e: "4 → 0100, 7 → 0111.", d: 2 },
    { q: "The key property of Gray code is that:", o: ["It uses 8 bits", "Successive values differ in only one bit", "It encodes letters", "It is used only in IBM mainframes"], a: 1, e: "That single-bit change minimises errors during transitions.", d: 2 },
    { q: "What is the Gray code of binary 0110 (decimal 6)?", o: ["0101", "0110", "0111", "0100"], a: 0, e: "0110 XOR 0011 = 0101.", d: 3 },
    { q: "How many bytes does UTF-8 use for the rupee sign ₹ (U+20B9)?", o: ["1", "2", "3", "4"], a: 2, e: "U+0800–U+FFFF needs 3 bytes in UTF-8: E2 82 B9.", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-ascii", unit: "u3", title: "ASCII — American Standard Code for Information Interchange", minutes: 9,
  summary: "The 7-bit character code behind almost all text: the table structure, extended ASCII, and how text is encoded, transmitted and decoded.",
  source: "Unit III slides 15–20", related: ["u3-ebcdic", "u3-codes"],
  intro: "<p>When you type “Hi” and press Send, your keyboard and computer turn each letter into a number using <b>ASCII</b>. Every PC, phone and network device understands it.</p>",
  definition: "ASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) is a character-encoding standard that assigns unique numerical values to letters, digits, punctuation marks and control characters, enabling computers to store, process and exchange text. Standard ASCII uses 7 bits (128 characters).",
  why: "<p>ASCII is the foundation of text-based data communication. It gives systems a common language, ensures compatibility between different systems, and is the basis of Unicode (UTF-8 is backward-compatible with ASCII).</p>",
  how: "<h3>History</h3><p>Developed in the early 1960s by the American Standards Association (ASA), ASCII grew out of <b>telegraph code</b> and <b>Morse code</b>. It became a standard way to represent characters in computers and to interchange data between them.</p>" +
    "<h3>Key aspects</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Character representation</b> — a unique 7-bit (or 8-bit) code per character.</li><li><b>Standardisation</b> — a common language between systems.</li><li><b>Text-based data</b> — text files, email and transmission protocols.</li><li><b>Basic set</b> — 7-bit ASCII defines <b>128 characters</b>: upper- and lowercase letters, digits, punctuation and control characters.</li><li><b>Extended ASCII</b> — 8 bits, adding 128 more symbols and language-specific characters.</li><li><b>Foundation</b> — ASCII is the basis of Unicode.</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>How ASCII works in data communication</h3><ol class='points'><li><b>Encoding</b> — the sender converts each character into its ASCII value.</li><li><b>Transmission</b> — the values travel over the channel (cable, Internet) as bits.</li><li><b>Decoding</b> — the receiver converts the values back to characters using the ASCII table.</li></ol>" +
    "<div class='codebox'>'A' = 65 = 0100 0001        'a' = 97 = 0110 0001\n'1' = 49 = 0011 0001        ' ' = 32 = 0010 0000\n\"Hi\" → 72 105 → 01001000 01101001</div>",
  deep: "<div class='table-wrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Range (decimal)</th><th>Hex</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody>" +
    "<tr><td>0–31, 127</td><td>00–1F, 7F</td><td>Control characters: NUL, SOH, STX, ETX, EOT, ENQ, ACK, BEL, BS, HT, LF, CR, ESC, DEL…</td></tr>" +
    "<tr><td>32</td><td>20</td><td>Space</td></tr><tr><td>48–57</td><td>30–39</td><td>Digits 0–9</td></tr><tr><td>65–90</td><td>41–5A</td><td>Uppercase A–Z</td></tr><tr><td>97–122</td><td>61–7A</td><td>Lowercase a–z</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" +
    "<p><b>Clever structure:</b> uppercase and lowercase letters differ by exactly 32 — only <b>bit 5</b> changes (A = 100 0001, a = 110 0001). Digit values are the digit plus 48 (0x30), so '7' − '0' = 7. Letters are contiguous, so A–Z sort correctly by code.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Control characters in communication.</b> STX/ETX mark the start and end of text, ACK/NAK acknowledge, SYN synchronises, and EOT ends transmission — names you will meet again in data-link protocols. CR + LF end a line on Windows and in many Internet protocols (HTTP, SMTP).</p>" +
    "<p><b>The 8th bit.</b> On 8-bit links the spare bit was historically used as a <b>parity bit</b> for error detection, or for extended characters (ISO 8859-1, code page 437).</p>",
  examples: ["Text files (.txt) and source code", "Email headers and HTTP request lines", "AT commands sent to modems", "Serial terminal communication (UART)"],
  terms: [["Control character", "Non-printing code such as LF, CR, ACK or ESC (0–31, 127)."], ["Extended ASCII", "8-bit sets adding 128 extra characters."], ["Code page", "A specific mapping of the upper 128 codes."], ["ASA", "American Standards Association, which created ASCII (now ANSI)."]],
  exam: ["ASCII assigns unique numerical values to characters for storage, processing and exchange.", "Standard ASCII: 7 bits, 128 characters; extended ASCII: 8 bits, 256 characters.", "Developed early 1960s; origins in telegraph and Morse code.", "Process: encoding → transmission → decoding using the ASCII table.", "'A' = 65 = 01000001; '1' = 49.", "ASCII forms the basis of Unicode."],
  recap: "ASCII = 7-bit text code (128 characters). A = 65, a = 97, 0 = 48, space = 32. Encode → transmit → decode.",
  viz: { id: "v-ascii", intro: "Characters are typed, looked up in the ASCII table, turned into bits and sent." },
  sim: { id: "s-ascii", tasks: ["Type your name and read the decimal, hex and binary codes.", "Compare 'A' and 'a'. Which single bit differs?", "Turn on the parity bit option and see how the 8th bit is chosen for each character."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "Standard ASCII uses how many bits per character?", o: ["4", "7", "16", "32"], a: 1, e: "Standard ASCII is a 7-bit code (128 characters).", d: 1 },
    { q: "The ASCII value of 'A' is:", o: ["41", "65", "97", "48"], a: 1, e: "'A' = 65 decimal = 0x41.", d: 1 },
    { q: "How many characters does extended (8-bit) ASCII support?", o: ["128", "256", "512", "65,536"], a: 1, e: "2⁸ = 256.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Which correctly orders the steps of ASCII in data communication?", o: ["Decoding → transmission → encoding", "Encoding → transmission → decoding", "Transmission → encoding → decoding", "Encoding → decoding → transmission"], a: 1, e: "The sender encodes, the channel transmits, the receiver decodes.", d: 2 },
    { q: "'a' is 97 in ASCII. What is 'd'?", o: ["98", "99", "100", "68"], a: 2, e: "Letters are contiguous: a = 97, b = 98, c = 99, d = 100.", d: 3 },
    { q: "Which single bit differs between an uppercase letter and its lowercase form in ASCII?", o: ["Bit 0 (value 1)", "Bit 5 (value 32)", "Bit 7 (value 128)", "Bit 3 (value 8)"], a: 1, e: "They differ by 32, i.e. bit 5.", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-ebcdic", unit: "u3", title: "EBCDIC and ASCII vs EBCDIC", minutes: 9,
  summary: "IBM's 8-bit Extended Binary Coded Decimal Interchange Code — its zone/digit structure, examples, advantages, disadvantages — and how it compares with ASCII.",
  source: "Unit III slides 7–14",
  intro: "<p>Banks and airlines still run IBM mainframes that do not use ASCII. They use <b>EBCDIC</b>, an 8-bit code with a completely different bit pattern for every letter.</p>",
  definition: "EBCDIC (Extended Binary Coded Decimal Interchange Code) is an 8-bit character-encoding system developed by IBM. With 8 bits it can assign the values 0–255 (256 codes) to alphabetic, numeric, punctuation, control and special characters.",
  why: "<p>EBCDIC is still used on IBM mainframes, in legacy banking systems and in COBOL business applications. Data exchanged between a mainframe and a PC must be translated between EBCDIC and ASCII, or it turns into gibberish.</p>",
  how: "<p>EBCDIC improved on the older 6-bit BCD code (BCDIC). It was introduced with IBM mainframe systems in <b>1964</b> (System/360) and lets machines store and transmit text efficiently in 8-bit form. ASCII is now dominant, but some mainframes still use EBCDIC.</p>" +
    "<h3>Key features</h3><div class='table-wrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bit length</td><td>8 bits (1 byte)</td></tr><tr><td>Total characters</td><td>256 (2⁸)</td></tr><tr><td>Developer</td><td>IBM</td></tr><tr><td>Primary use</td><td>IBM mainframes and legacy systems</td></tr><tr><td>Character set</td><td>Letters, digits, punctuation, control characters</td></tr><tr><td>Incompatibility</td><td>Different bit patterns from ASCII for the same character</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" +
    "<h3>Structure: zones</h3><p>Each byte is a <b>zone</b> nibble (high 4 bits) plus a <b>digit</b> nibble (low 4 bits):</p><div class='codebox'>Zone 1100 (C): A–I → C1–C9        Zone 1101 (D): J–R → D1–D9\nZone 1110 (E): S–Z → E2–E9        Zone 1111 (F): 0–9 → F0–F9\n9 + 9 + 8 + 10 = 36 codes for letters and digits, out of 2⁸ = 256 possible\n\nA = 1100 0001 (C1)   B = 1100 0010 (C2)   0 = 1111 0000 (F0)   space = 0100 0000 (40)</div>" +
    "<div class='note example'><span class='note-title'>Worked example (slide 12): write the EBCDIC code for “BIT”</span><p>B = 1100 0010, I = 1100 1001, T = 1110 0011 → <code>11000010 11001001 11100011</code>. Three characters need <b>3 bytes</b>.</p></div>" +
    "<h3>Advantages</h3><ul class='points'><li>Supports legacy systems</li><li>Prioritises reliability</li><li>Encoding longevity</li><li>Efficient use of storage</li><li>Suits specialised processing needs</li></ul><h3>Disadvantages</h3><ul class='points'><li>Lack of modern hardware compatibility</li><li>Additional translation overhead</li><li>Scarcity of knowledgeable talent</li><li>Security and support concerns</li><li>Migration complexity</li></ul><h3>Applications</h3><p>IBM mainframes, legacy banking systems, COBOL and other business applications.</p>",
  compare: { head: ["ASCII", "EBCDIC"], rows: [["American Standard Code for Information Interchange; created by ASA", "Extended Binary Coded Decimal Interchange Code; created by IBM"], ["Used in desktops, mobiles and laptops", "Used mostly in IBM mainframes"], ["7 bits per character — more efficient", "8 bits per character — less efficient"], ["Up to 128 characters (256 extended)", "Up to 256 characters"], ["Letters are contiguous (A–Z = 65–90)", "Letters are split into zones with gaps (A–I, J–R, S–Z)"], ["Works with Unicode (UTF-8 is ASCII-compatible)", "Not directly compatible with Unicode"], ["Origin: ANSI (ASA)", "Origin: IBM"]] },
  deep: "<p><b>Why the gaps?</b> EBCDIC descends from punched cards. A card column combined a <i>zone punch</i> with a <i>digit punch</i>, and the letter groups A–I, J–R and S–Z correspond to the three zone rows. On cards, S–Z used the zero-zone with digit punches 2–9, because the 0-1 punch combination was already taken by “/”. EBCDIC kept that layout, so S starts at E2 rather than E1. As a result, code arithmetic such as “next letter = code + 1” fails at I→J and R→S, unlike ASCII.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Code pages.</b> EBCDIC has variants: CP037 (US/Canada) and CP500 (international) differ for some punctuation such as [ ] and !. This is one more reason translation is error-prone. Mainframe file transfers (FTP in ASCII mode) translate automatically; binary mode does not.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Collation.</b> In EBCDIC, lowercase letters (81–A9) sort <i>before</i> uppercase (C1–E9), and letters sort before digits (F0–F9). In ASCII, digits come first, then uppercase, then lowercase. Sorted reports therefore differ between platforms.</p>",
  examples: ["Bank core systems on IBM Z mainframes", "Airline reservation systems", "COBOL payroll programs", "Converting mainframe reports for PCs (EBCDIC → ASCII)"],
  terms: [["Zone", "High nibble of an EBCDIC byte (C, D, E, F for letters/digits)."], ["Digit nibble", "Low 4 bits of an EBCDIC byte."], ["BCDIC", "Older 6-bit code that EBCDIC extended."], ["Code page", "A specific EBCDIC variant (e.g. CP037)."]],
  exam: ["EBCDIC: 8 bits, 256 codes, developed by IBM, used in IBM mainframes since 1964.", "Improved on the older 6-bit BCD code.", "Zones: A–I C1–C9, J–R D1–D9, S–Z E2–E9, 0–9 F0–F9 (36 codes).", "BIT = 11000010 11001001 11100011 (3 bytes).", "ASCII vs EBCDIC: ASA vs IBM; 7 vs 8 bits; 128 vs 256; PCs vs mainframes; Unicode-compatible vs not."],
  recap: "EBCDIC = IBM's 8-bit code with zone + digit nibbles (A = C1, 0 = F0, space = 40). ASCII = 7-bit, contiguous, universal. Same letter, different bits — translation needed.",
  viz: { id: "v-ebcdic", intro: "Watch a character split into its zone and digit nibbles, then compare it with ASCII." },
  sim: { id: "s-ebcdic", tasks: ["Encode “BIT” and check the result against the slide example.", "Send ASCII bytes to a receiver that decodes them as EBCDIC. What does it display?", "Find the gap: compare the codes of I and J, and of R and S."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "EBCDIC was developed by:", o: ["ANSI", "IBM", "ISO", "Microsoft"], a: 1, e: "IBM developed EBCDIC for its mainframes.", d: 1 },
    { q: "How many bits does EBCDIC use per character?", o: ["6", "7", "8", "16"], a: 2, e: "EBCDIC is an 8-bit code.", d: 1 },
    { q: "The EBCDIC code for 'A' is:", o: ["41", "C1", "F1", "65"], a: 1, e: "A = 1100 0001 = C1 hex.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Which statement correctly compares ASCII and EBCDIC?", o: ["Both use 8 bits", "ASCII is used in IBM mainframes", "ASCII uses 7 bits; EBCDIC uses 8 bits", "EBCDIC is used in mobiles"], a: 2, e: "ASCII 7-bit (PCs); EBCDIC 8-bit (mainframes).", d: 2 },
    { q: "How many bytes are needed to store the word “DATA” in EBCDIC?", o: ["2", "4", "8", "32"], a: 1, e: "One byte per character: 4 bytes.", d: 3 },
    { q: "The EBCDIC code of S is E2, not E1. This shows that EBCDIC:", o: ["Is contiguous like ASCII", "Has gaps between letter groups inherited from punched cards", "Is a 7-bit code", "Uses Unicode"], a: 1, e: "EBCDIC letters are split into zones with gaps (I→J, R→S).", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-voice", unit: "u3", title: "Voice Channel Communications", minutes: 8,
  summary: "Sending data over channels built for voice calls: the voice band, data-in-voice tones, VoIP, advantages and examples.",
  source: "Unit III slides 21–25",
  intro: "<p>The telephone network was built to carry <b>voice</b>. Data communication learned to reuse those same voice channels — from dial-up modems to tones on mobile calls — and later voice moved onto data networks (VoIP).</p>",
  definition: "Voice channel communication, in data communication, means transmitting data over the channels normally used for voice calls, such as the traditional telephone network or the voice channels of a wireless network.",
  why: "<p>Voice channels are everywhere and are reliable. Using them lets devices send data where no dedicated data network exists, or send data discreetly inside a voice signal.</p>",
  how: "<h3>How voice chat works</h3><ol class='points'><li><b>Capture</b> — the microphone records the speaker's voice.</li><li><b>Transmit</b> — the voice is digitised and sent as packets.</li><li><b>Process</b> — servers on the Internet route it.</li><li><b>Receive</b> — the other device receives the packets.</li><li><b>Playback</b> — the speaker plays the voice.</li></ol>" +
    "<h3>Data communication over voice channels</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>VoIP (Voice over Internet Protocol)</b> converts voice into data packets sent over the Internet. Strictly this is <i>voice over a data network</i>, the reverse of data over a voice channel.</li><li><b>Data in a voice channel</b> — data is encoded into <b>tones</b> and sent through a voice call, as in some mobile communication devices (data-over-voice, DoV).</li></ul>" +
    "<p>A secure data-over-voice chain (slide 23): speech encoder → speech enciphering → error protection → DoV data modulator → <b>voice channel</b> → DoV demodulator → error correction → deciphering → speech decoder.</p>" +
    "<h3>Advantages</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Reliability</b> — voice channels provide a stable communication path.</li><li><b>Transparency</b> — data can be sent without changing the network infrastructure.</li><li><b>Security</b> — data in a voice-like format can be more discreet.</li></ul><h3>Examples</h3><ul class='points'><li>Mobile devices encoding data into tones over a wireless voice channel</li><li>Older systems using a modem over a phone line</li><li>Voice-dedicated networks that also carry data</li></ul>",
  deep: "<p><b>The voice channel.</b> Telephone speech is band-limited to about <b>300–3400 Hz</b>, carried in a nominal <b>4 kHz</b> channel that includes guard space. Anything outside that band is filtered out, so data must be shaped into tones inside it. That is why modems use audio tones (e.g. Bell 202: 1200 Hz mark, 2200 Hz space).</p>" +
    "<div class='codebox'>Digitising a voice channel (PCM, the DS0 channel):\n  Nyquist sampling ≥ 2 × 4 kHz = 8000 samples/s\n  8 bits per sample × 8000 samples/s = 64 kbit/s per voice channel\n(24 such channels + framing = the T1 carrier in Unit IV)</div>" +
    "<p><b>DTMF</b> (touch-tone dialling) is data in a voice channel: each key sends two simultaneous tones, one from {697, 770, 852, 941 Hz} and one from {1209, 1336, 1477 Hz}. The exchange decodes the pair into a digit.</p>" +
    "<p><b>VoIP codecs</b> compress voice: G.711 (64 kbit/s, uncompressed PCM), G.729 (8 kbit/s), Opus (6–510 kbit/s, adaptive).</p>",
  examples: ["Dial-up Internet: data tones over a phone call", "DTMF: pressing “1” for customer service menus", "WhatsApp/Zoom calls: voice over IP", "eCall emergency systems sending vehicle position as tones in a voice call"],
  terms: [["Voice band", "About 300–3400 Hz, the frequencies a phone line passes."], ["VoIP", "Voice carried as IP packets over a data network."], ["Data over voice (DoV)", "Data encoded as tones inside a voice channel."], ["DTMF", "Dual-tone multi-frequency signalling for keypad digits."], ["PCM", "Pulse-code modulation: sample, quantise, encode voice."]],
  exam: ["Definition: transmitting data over channels used for voice calls (telephone network, wireless voice channels).", "VoIP: voice converted to packets over the Internet (voice over data, not data over voice).", "Data in voice channel: data encoded into tones over the voice channel.", "Advantages: reliability, transparency, security.", "Examples: mobile devices (tones), older systems (modems), voice-dedicated networks."],
  recap: "Data over voice = tones inside the 300–3400 Hz voice band. VoIP = the reverse: voice as data packets.",
  viz: { id: "v-voice", intro: "See voice captured, digitised, packetised and played back — and data tones placed inside the voice band." },
  sim: { id: "s-voiceband", tasks: ["Move a tone from 100 Hz to 5000 Hz. Which frequencies pass the voice channel?", "Select a DTMF key and see its two tones inside the band.", "Use “Play” to listen to the tone (sound on)."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "Voice channel communication means transmitting data over:", o: ["Fibre backbones only", "Channels normally used for voice calls", "Satellite TV channels", "USB cables"], a: 1, e: "It reuses voice channels for data.", d: 1 },
    { q: "VoIP stands for:", o: ["Voice over Internet Protocol", "Video over IP", "Voice output interface port", "Very old Internet phone"], a: 0, e: "VoIP = Voice over Internet Protocol.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Which is listed as an advantage of data over voice channels?", o: ["Unlimited bandwidth", "Reliability of voice channels", "No need for modulation", "Uses infrared"], a: 1, e: "Advantages: reliability, transparency, security.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Why is VoIP not strictly “data over a voice channel”?", o: ["It uses fibre", "It carries voice over a data network — the reverse direction", "It is analog", "It has no packets"], a: 1, e: "VoIP turns voice into data packets on the Internet.", d: 2 },
    { q: "A telephone voice channel passes roughly:", o: ["0–20 kHz", "300–3400 Hz", "1–2 GHz", "50–60 Hz"], a: 1, e: "Telephone speech is band-limited to about 300–3400 Hz.", d: 3 },
    { q: "Digitising one voice channel with PCM (8000 samples/s, 8 bits/sample) gives:", o: ["8 kbit/s", "32 kbit/s", "64 kbit/s", "1.544 Mbit/s"], a: 2, e: "8000 × 8 = 64,000 bit/s (a DS0 channel).", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-telephone", unit: "u3", title: "Telephone Networks and POTS", minutes: 9,
  summary: "The circuit-switched telephone network, its three major components (local loops, trunks, switching offices) and the Plain Old Telephone Service.",
  source: "Unit III slides 26–34",
  intro: "<p>The telephone network is the world's oldest large communication network. It was built for voice using <b>circuit switching</b>, and it later learned to carry data. Its original form is called <b>POTS</b>, the Plain Old Telephone Service.</p>",
  definition: "A telephone network provides voice communication using circuit switching. POTS (Plain Old Telephone Service, also expanded as Plain Old Telephone Set) is the traditional analog telephone system that carries voice over copper wires, and it can carry data using a modem.",
  why: "<p>The telephone network's local loops, exchanges and trunks became the first infrastructure for data communication (dial-up, DSL). Its hierarchy and impairments explain much of Unit III.</p>",
  how: "<p>The whole network was originally the analog POTS. With the computer era it gained the ability to carry data as well as voice; today's network is both <b>analog and digital</b>.</p>" +
    "<h3>Major components</h3><ol class='points'><li><b>Local loops</b> — twisted-pair cables connecting a subscriber's telephone to the nearest end office (local central office). For voice, the bandwidth is <b>4000 Hz</b>. In a 7-digit number, the first three digits identify the office and the next four identify the local loop.</li><li><b>Trunks</b> — transmission media handling communication <i>between offices</i>. Through multiplexing, a trunk carries hundreds or thousands of connections, mainly over optical fibre or satellite links.</li><li><b>Switching offices</b> — rather than a permanent physical link between every pair of subscribers, the company uses <b>switches</b> in switching offices that connect loops or trunks on demand. Levels: <b>end offices</b>, <b>tandem offices</b> and <b>regional offices</b>.</li></ol>" +
    "<h3>POTS</h3><p>The traditional analog system carrying voice over copper. It mainly carries voice but can carry data with a modem, typically up to <b>56 kbit/s</b>, by converting the computer's digital signals into analog signals.</p><p><b>Example:</b> with a dial-up modem, your computer's digital data is converted to analog, sent over the phone line, and converted back to digital at the ISP.</p>" +
    "<h3>How a POTS call travels (slide 34)</h3><ol class='points'><li>The handset converts the caller's voice into electrical signals, which travel over a <b>drop cable</b> to an outside terminal.</li><li>The terminal consolidates signals from the neighbourhood and sends them to the <b>central office</b>.</li><li>Computerised switches in the central office decide where to route the call.</li><li>The call may go via a regional hub (<b>tandem office</b>) to a distant central office, or directly to a central office near the destination.</li><li>The destination central office routes the call to the right terminal and local line.</li><li>The receiving handset converts the electrical signal back into sound.</li></ol>" +
    "<h3>Advantages, disadvantages and applications</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Advantages:</b> circuit-switched; no transmission delay once connected because any receiver can be selected; cheap because the network is widespread.</li><li><b>Disadvantages:</b> takes a long time to set up a connection; low transmission speed.</li><li><b>Applications:</b> connecting people; business advertising; recreation worldwide.</li></ul>",
  deep: "<p><b>Loop signalling on a POTS line.</b> The exchange powers the line with about <b>−48 V DC</b>. Lifting the handset (<i>off-hook</i>) draws current and the exchange returns a dial tone. Digits are sent as pulses or DTMF tones. To ring a phone, the exchange sends an AC ringing signal of about <b>20 Hz</b> (roughly 75–90 V). That same 20 Hz ringing appears later as a cause of amplitude jitter.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Two-wire to four-wire.</b> The local loop is a single pair carrying both directions. Inside the network, each direction gets its own path (four-wire). A <b>hybrid</b> circuit converts between them, and an imperfect hybrid is the main source of <b>echo</b>, another Unit III impairment.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Circuit switching.</b> A dedicated 64 kbit/s path (one DS0 slot in TDM trunks) is reserved for the whole call, even during silence. That gives constant delay and no queuing, but wastes capacity compared with packet switching.</p>",
  examples: ["Landline phones in homes and offices", "Dial-up Internet over POTS", "Fax machines", "Lift and alarm emergency lines (still commonly POTS)"],
  terms: [["POTS", "Plain Old Telephone Service — the analog voice network."], ["Trunk", "Multiplexed line between switching offices."], ["End office", "Local exchange where subscriber loops terminate."], ["Tandem office", "Exchange connecting other exchanges (no subscribers)."], ["Drop cable", "Wire from the house to the street terminal."], ["Hybrid", "Converts 2-wire loops to 4-wire trunks."]],
  exam: ["Telephone network provides voice using circuit switching; originally POTS (analog), now analog + digital.", "Three components: local loops, trunks, switching offices.", "Local loop: twisted pair to the end office; voice bandwidth 4000 Hz; first 3 digits = office, next 4 = loop.", "Trunks: inter-office, multiplexed, fibre or satellite.", "Switching offices: end, tandem and regional offices.", "POTS carries data via modem up to 56 kbit/s."],
  recap: "Phone network = local loops (to the exchange) + trunks (between exchanges) + switching offices (end → tandem → regional). POTS = analog voice on copper; data via modem.",
  viz: { id: "v-pots", intro: "Follow a call from off-hook and dial tone through switching to ringing and conversation." },
  sim: { id: "s-dial", tasks: ["Dial a number in the same office (555-xxxx). How many switches are used?", "Dial a number with a different office code. Which trunk does the call take?", "Explain how the first three digits are used by the network."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "The telephone network uses which type of switching?", o: ["Packet switching", "Circuit switching", "Message switching", "No switching"], a: 1, e: "Telephone networks are circuit-switched.", d: 1 },
    { q: "POTS stands for:", o: ["Packet Over Telephone System", "Plain Old Telephone Service", "Public Optical Transmission System", "Point Of Telephone Switching"], a: 1, e: "Plain Old Telephone Service (slide also says “Set”).", d: 1 },
    { q: "Which component carries communication BETWEEN telephone offices?", o: ["Local loop", "Trunk", "Handset", "Drop cable"], a: 1, e: "Trunks connect offices and are multiplexed.", d: 2 },
    { q: "In a 7-digit telephone number, the first three digits identify the:", o: ["Local loop", "Office (exchange)", "Country", "Trunk"], a: 1, e: "First three = office; last four = local loop.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Why can POTS deliver at most about 56 kbit/s of data?", o: ["Copper cannot carry electricity", "Data must fit inside the narrow ~4 kHz voice channel", "It uses fibre", "It uses EBCDIC"], a: 1, e: "The voice-band limit restricts the modem's data rate.", d: 3 },
    { q: "The circuit that converts a 2-wire local loop to the 4-wire network inside the exchange is the:", o: ["Repeater", "Hybrid", "Modem", "Multiplexer"], a: 1, e: "Hybrids do 2-to-4-wire conversion; a mismatch causes echo.", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-hierarchy", unit: "u3", title: "Local Loop, Central Office and Hierarchical Phone Networks", minutes: 9,
  summary: "The last-mile local loop (copper, fibre, wireless), what a central office does, and how calls climb and descend the switching hierarchy.",
  source: "Unit III slides 35–45", related: ["u1-telecom"],
  intro: "<p>A call from Dhanore to Mumbai does not use a wire running directly between the two phones. It climbs a <b>hierarchy</b> of switching offices and comes back down. This topic follows that journey.</p>",
  definition: "The local loop is the physical connection between a customer's premises and the telephone company's central office (end office or exchange). A central office is the telecom facility that terminates local loops and switches and aggregates traffic. A hierarchical phone network arranges switches in levels — end offices, toll/tandem offices and regional offices.",
  why: "<p>The hierarchy explains how millions of phones connect with a manageable number of trunks. The central office is where voice and broadband services begin, and today it is turning into a data centre.</p>",
  how: "<h3>Local loop</h3><p>The <b>“last mile”</b> connecting home or business to the service provider. Traditionally twisted-pair copper (typically up to about 5 km to the central office); now also fibre or wireless. It enables voice and data: POTS, DSL and ISDN.</p><ul class='points'><li><b>Why it matters:</b> it is the foundation for phone, Internet and data services. <b>Local loop unbundling (LLU)</b> lets different providers use the same physical loop, which promotes competition.</li><li><b>Types:</b> <b>copper-based</b> (voice and DSL), <b>fibre-optic</b> (higher bandwidth), <b>wireless</b> (radio — Wireless Local Loop, WLL).</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>Central office (CO)</h3><p>A hub for managing and distributing network connections for voice, data and broadband. It houses optical distribution frames (ODFs), fibre panels and switching systems. Key functions:</p><ul class='points'><li><b>Switching</b> — routes calls and data between users and networks.</li><li><b>Aggregation</b> — combines traffic from many local lines towards regional and long-distance networks.</li><li><b>Connectivity</b> — physical termination point for local loops.</li><li><b>Service provisioning</b> — delivers voice, broadband and data services.</li><li><b>Network management</b> — monitors performance and resources.</li></ul><p>Modern COs are being <b>virtualised</b>: proprietary hardware is replaced by servers for the control and data planes, with hardware acceleration.</p>" +
    "<h3>Hierarchical structure</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>End offices</b> — local switches connecting individual subscribers.</li><li><b>Toll / tandem offices</b> — higher-level switches routing calls between end offices, often within a metropolitan area.</li><li><b>Regional offices</b> — top-level switches for long-distance and international calls.</li><li><b>Data Communication Network (DCN)</b> — connects network elements to the management system for operations, administration and maintenance.</li></ul>" +
    "<div class='note example'><span class='note-title'>Example (slide 45): a call from Dhanore to Mumbai</span><p>Phone in Dhanore → Dhanore <b>end office</b> → <b>toll office</b> (e.g. Pune) → Mumbai toll office → recipient's <b>end office</b> in Mumbai → recipient's phone.</p></div>",
  deep: "<p><b>The classic five-level hierarchy</b> (North American Bell System): Class 5 end office → Class 4 toll centre → Class 3 primary centre → Class 2 sectional centre → Class 1 regional centre. A call climbs only as high as needed to find a common parent, and <i>high-usage trunks</i> provide shortcuts between busy offices.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Why a hierarchy?</b> Connecting N exchanges directly needs N(N−1)/2 trunk groups (a mesh). For 1000 exchanges that is 499,500 trunk groups; a tree needs only about N. The cost is longer paths and dependence on higher-level offices.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Signalling.</b> Modern networks set up calls with out-of-band <b>SS7</b> signalling between switches, and traffic between offices is carried on TDM (T1/E1 and SONET/SDH) or IP trunks.</p>",
  examples: ["Your landline or DSL connecting to the nearest telephone exchange", "FTTH: a fibre local loop to an OLT in the central office", "Long-distance call routed through toll offices", "Operators sharing BSNL copper loops through unbundling"],
  terms: [["Last mile", "The local loop from the customer to the network."], ["LLU", "Local loop unbundling: rivals lease the incumbent's loops."], ["WLL", "Wireless local loop."], ["ODF", "Optical distribution frame for fibre termination."], ["Toll / tandem office", "Switch connecting end offices."], ["SS7", "Signalling system used between exchanges."]],
  exam: ["Local loop = physical link from customer premises to the central office; the “last mile”; copper, fibre or wireless (WLL); supports POTS, DSL, ISDN.", "LLU lets multiple providers share the loop.", "Central office functions: switching, aggregation, connectivity, service provisioning, network management.", "Hierarchy: end offices → toll/tandem offices → regional offices; DCN for OAM.", "Dhanore → Pune toll → Mumbai example."],
  recap: "Local loop to the CO; COs switch and aggregate; calls climb end → toll → regional only as far as needed, then come back down.",
  viz: { id: "v-hierarchy", intro: "Watch a call climb the switching hierarchy and descend to the called party." },
  sim: { id: "s-callroute", tasks: ["Route a call between two phones on the same end office. How many offices are used?", "Route Dhanore → Mumbai. Which levels of the hierarchy are involved?", "Compare the number of trunk groups needed by a full mesh and by the hierarchy for 50 exchanges."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "The local loop connects the customer's premises to the:", o: ["Regional office", "Central office (end office)", "Satellite", "ISP backbone directly"], a: 1, e: "The local loop runs from the subscriber to the central office.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Which is NOT a type of local loop?", o: ["Copper-based", "Fibre-optic", "Wireless (WLL)", "Stripline"], a: 3, e: "Local loops are copper, fibre or wireless.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Local loop unbundling (LLU) allows:", o: ["Removing all copper lines", "Different service providers to use the same physical local loop", "Faster dial tones", "Encryption"], a: 1, e: "LLU promotes competition by sharing loops.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Which switches route calls between end offices, often within a metropolitan area?", o: ["Regional offices", "Toll/tandem offices", "Handsets", "Gateways"], a: 1, e: "Toll or tandem offices connect end offices.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Which central office function combines traffic from many local lines towards long-distance networks?", o: ["Switching", "Aggregation", "Provisioning", "Management"], a: 1, e: "Aggregation collects traffic from many lines.", d: 3 },
    { q: "Connecting 100 exchanges in a full mesh needs how many trunk groups?", o: ["100", "4,950", "10,000", "99"], a: 1, e: "N(N−1)/2 = 100 × 99 / 2 = 4950 — which is why networks use a hierarchy.", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-impairments", unit: "u3", title: "Telephone Line Characteristics and Line Impairments", minutes: 9,
  summary: "Properties of voice-grade telephone lines and the three causes of impairment — attenuation, distortion and noise — with their effects on data.",
  source: "Unit III slides 46–50",
  intro: "<p>A telephone line is far from a perfect wire. The signal comes out weaker, changed in shape and mixed with noise. These <b>line impairments</b> are what limit modem speeds and cause bit errors.</p>",
  definition: "Line impairments are unwanted disturbances or degradations during signal transmission that affect the accuracy and reliability of data — they distort, weaken or alter the original signal. The three main causes are attenuation, distortion and noise.",
  why: "<p>Understanding impairments explains bit errors and why techniques such as amplifiers, equalisers, shielding, error detection and robust modulation exist.</p>",
  how: "<h3>Telephone line characteristics</h3><ul class='points'><li>Designed for <b>voice</b>; used in early data communication with modems converting digital to analog.</li><li><b>Limited bandwidth</b>: about 300–3300 Hz for voice.</li><li>Can operate in <b>full-duplex</b> mode.</li><li>Uses <b>twisted-pair copper</b>, often unshielded.</li><li><b>Susceptible to noise and distortion</b>, which affects data quality.</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>1. Attenuation</h3><p><b>Definition:</b> loss of signal strength as it travels through the medium. <b>Cause:</b> resistance of the medium, distance, environmental factors. <b>Effect:</b> the signal weakens and may become too weak to receive reliably. (Fix: amplifiers or repeaters.)</p>" +
    "<h3>2. Distortion</h3><p><b>Definition:</b> the shape or form of the signal changes. <b>Cause:</b> different frequency components travel at different speeds and arrive at different times. <b>Effect:</b> the received signal differs from the original, so data is misinterpreted. (Fix: equalisers.)</p>" +
    "<h3>3. Noise</h3><p><b>Definition:</b> any unwanted signal interfering with the original. <b>Cause:</b> electromagnetic interference (EMI), crosstalk from nearby cables, thermal noise in electronic components. <b>Effect:</b> obscures the signal, so the intended data is hard to distinguish.</p>",
  deep: "<div class='codebox'>Attenuation (dB) = 10·log₁₀(P_in / P_out)\nSignal-to-noise ratio: SNR(dB) = 10·log₁₀(P_signal / P_noise)\nShannon capacity:      C = B · log₂(1 + SNR)\n\nVoice line: B ≈ 3100 Hz, SNR ≈ 30 dB (= 1000)\n  C ≈ 3100 × log₂(1001) ≈ 30.9 kbit/s  → why analog modems peaked at 28.8–33.6 kbit/s</div>" +
    "<p><b>Two kinds of distortion on phone lines:</b> <i>attenuation distortion</i> (different frequencies lose different amounts) and <i>delay (envelope) distortion</i> (different frequencies arrive at different times). Leased data lines were <b>conditioned</b> (C- and D-conditioning) to flatten both.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Noise types:</b> thermal (white) noise from electron motion, intermodulation from non-linear circuits, crosstalk from neighbouring pairs, and impulse noise (spikes). Thermal noise sets an unavoidable floor; the others can be reduced.</p>",
  examples: ["A long rural phone line where the far voice sounds faint (attenuation)", "Old modems dropping speed on a noisy line", "Hiss on an AM radio far from the station (noise)", "Square-looking data pulses that become rounded and smeared (distortion)"],
  terms: [["Attenuation", "Loss of signal power with distance."], ["Distortion", "Change in waveform shape."], ["Noise", "Unwanted added signal."], ["SNR", "Ratio of signal power to noise power."], ["Equaliser", "Circuit compensating for frequency-dependent loss or delay."]],
  exam: ["Telephone lines: voice-designed, ~300–3300 Hz bandwidth, full-duplex capable, UTP copper, susceptible to noise and distortion.", "Line impairments: unwanted disturbances affecting accuracy and reliability.", "Attenuation: loss of strength (resistance, distance, environment).", "Distortion: shape changes because frequencies travel at different speeds.", "Noise: unwanted signal (EMI, crosstalk, thermal)."],
  recap: "Attenuation weakens, distortion reshapes, noise adds. Their combined effect (low SNR) limits capacity: C = B log₂(1 + SNR).",
  viz: { id: "v-impair", intro: "One clean data waveform is attenuated, then distorted, then buried in noise." },
  sim: { id: "s-impair", tasks: ["Add 12 dB attenuation with no noise. Are the bits still decoded correctly?", "Now add noise. At what noise level do bit errors begin?", "Add distortion and watch the square pulses round off and smear into each other."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "Loss of signal strength as it travels through a medium is called:", o: ["Noise", "Attenuation", "Distortion", "Jitter"], a: 1, e: "Attenuation = loss of strength.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Which is NOT one of the three main causes of line impairment?", o: ["Attenuation", "Distortion", "Noise", "Encryption"], a: 3, e: "The three causes are attenuation, distortion and noise.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Distortion occurs mainly because:", o: ["The cable is too short", "Different frequency components travel at different speeds", "The receiver is off", "Of EBCDIC encoding"], a: 1, e: "Frequency components arrive at different times, changing the shape.", d: 2 },
    { q: "The typical voice bandwidth of a telephone line given in the slides is:", o: ["0–20 kHz", "300–3300 Hz", "1–2 MHz", "50–60 Hz"], a: 1, e: "About 300 Hz to 3300 Hz.", d: 2 },
    { q: "A signal enters a line at 10 mW and leaves at 1 mW. The attenuation is:", o: ["1 dB", "3 dB", "10 dB", "20 dB"], a: 2, e: "10·log₁₀(10/1) = 10 dB.", d: 3 },
    { q: "Using Shannon's formula, why do voice-line modems top out around 30 kbit/s?", o: ["Copper is too heavy", "Bandwidth ≈ 3.1 kHz and SNR ≈ 30 dB give C ≈ 31 kbit/s", "Modems use EBCDIC", "Lines are simplex"], a: 1, e: "C = 3100 × log₂(1001) ≈ 30.9 kbit/s.", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-crosstalk", unit: "u3", title: "Crosstalk", minutes: 9,
  summary: "How a signal on one cable leaks into a neighbouring cable: capacitive and inductive coupling, forward/backward, NEXT/FEXT, effects and prevention.",
  source: "Unit III slides 51–57",
  intro: "<p>Ever heard a faint second conversation on a landline call? That is <b>crosstalk</b>: a signal from one cable leaking into a cable running next to it.</p>",
  definition: "Crosstalk is a type of noise in which a signal transmitted on one cable interferes with and corrupts the signal on another cable in close proximity. It is induced by coupling between signals on parallel adjacent cables, through electrostatic (capacitive) or electromagnetic (inductive) induction.",
  why: "<p>Crosstalk is the main noise source inside multi-pair cables and on circuit boards. It limits how fast Ethernet and DSL can run and is a key reason for twisting and shielding.</p>",
  how: "<p>Every analog signal has an associated electric or magnetic field. When cables run parallel, these fields overlap and <b>induce interference signals</b> in the neighbouring cable.</p>" +
    "<h3>Types of coupling</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Electrostatic (capacitive) coupling</b> — the electric field around a wire induces a signal in a parallel wire through the virtual capacitance between them. The result is <i>capacitive crosstalk</i>.</li><li><b>Electromagnetic (inductive) coupling</b> — the changing magnetic field of an alternating current induces a signal in a parallel wire through mutual inductance. The result is <i>inductive crosstalk</i>.</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>Classification by direction of propagation</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Forward crosstalk</b> — travels in the <i>same</i> direction as the disturbing signal.</li><li><b>Backward crosstalk</b> — travels in the <i>opposite</i> direction to the disturbing signal.</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>Classification by site of measurement</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Near-End Crosstalk (NEXT)</b> — disturbance at the <b>near end</b>, close to the source. The outgoing signal leaks into the incoming signal at the transmitting station.</li><li><b>Far-End Crosstalk (FEXT)</b> — disturbance at the <b>far end</b>, close to the destination. The leaked signal corrupts the other pair at the receiver end.</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>Effects</h3><p>Signal integrity issues, noise-on-delay effect, logic faults, voltage overshoot and timing noise.</p>" +
    "<h3>Prevention</h3><ul class='points'><li>Reduce the length of parallel wires.</li><li>Use signals of equal magnitude and opposite polarity (differential signalling).</li><li>Use twisted pairs with more turns per unit length (higher twist rate).</li><li>Replace UTP with STP.</li><li>Convert analog signals to digital before transmission.</li><li>Increase the distance between adjacent parallel cables.</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>Challenges and solutions</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Communication systems:</b> information leaks from one line to another → increase the twisting frequency.</li><li><b>Networking:</b> poor SNR and data loss → use STP instead of UTP.</li><li><b>Printed circuit boards:</b> coupling between traces → increase trace spacing, use differential signals, avoid parallel traces.</li></ul>",
  deep: "<p><b>Why NEXT is usually worse than FEXT.</b> At the near end, the disturbing signal is at full strength while the wanted incoming signal has already been attenuated by the whole cable length. At the far end, the disturber has been attenuated too. So NEXT is the critical specification for Ethernet cabling.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Crosstalk in dB.</b> Cable specifications quote NEXT loss in dB — how far <i>below</i> the signal the coupled crosstalk is, so a <b>higher number is better</b>. Coupling grows with frequency and with parallel length. <b>ACR</b> (attenuation-to-crosstalk ratio) = NEXT loss − attenuation; the link works while ACR is comfortably positive. <i>Alien crosstalk</i> comes from pairs in a <i>different</i> neighbouring cable.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Why different twist rates?</b> Each pair in a Cat 6 cable has a different twist length, so pairs do not stay aligned and their coupling averages out.</p>",
  examples: ["Faint voices from another call on old telephone cables", "Gigabit Ethernet errors on poor-quality cable (NEXT)", "DSL speed drops when many neighbours' lines are active in the same cable (FEXT)", "Glitches between closely spaced PCB traces"],
  terms: [["Capacitive coupling", "Interference through electric-field capacitance between wires."], ["Inductive coupling", "Interference through mutual inductance (magnetic field)."], ["NEXT", "Near-end crosstalk — measured at the transmitting end."], ["FEXT", "Far-end crosstalk — measured at the receiving end."], ["Victim / aggressor", "The disturbed signal / the disturbing signal."]],
  exam: ["Crosstalk: signal on one cable corrupts a signal on a nearby cable.", "Caused by electrostatic (capacitive) or electromagnetic (inductive) coupling between parallel cables.", "By direction: forward and backward crosstalk.", "By site: NEXT (near end, near the source) and FEXT (far end, near the destination).", "Prevention: shorter parallel runs, differential signals, more twists, STP, digital conversion, more spacing."],
  recap: "Neighbouring wires couple through electric (capacitive) and magnetic (inductive) fields. NEXT at the sender's end, FEXT at the receiver's end. Twist, shield, separate.",
  viz: { id: "v-crosstalk", intro: "An aggressor pair couples into a victim pair; watch NEXT appear at the near end and FEXT at the far end." },
  sim: { id: "s-crosstalk", tasks: ["Raise the signal frequency. What happens to NEXT and FEXT?", "Move the cables further apart, then add twists. Which helps more?", "Turn on shielding (STP) and compare the crosstalk in dB."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "Crosstalk occurs when:", o: ["A signal is reflected to the source", "A signal on one cable interferes with a nearby cable", "A cable is cut", "Data is encrypted"], a: 1, e: "Crosstalk is interference between adjacent cables.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Crosstalk measured at the end closest to the source is called:", o: ["FEXT", "NEXT", "Echo", "Jitter"], a: 1, e: "Near-End Crosstalk (NEXT).", d: 1 },
    { q: "Crosstalk caused by mutual inductance between wires is:", o: ["Capacitive crosstalk", "Inductive crosstalk", "Thermal noise", "Impulse noise"], a: 1, e: "Electromagnetic (inductive) coupling gives inductive crosstalk.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Which is a recommended way to reduce crosstalk in networking?", o: ["Use longer parallel cables", "Replace UTP with STP", "Use fewer twists", "Increase the signal frequency"], a: 1, e: "Shielded twisted pair reduces crosstalk.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Backward crosstalk propagates:", o: ["In the same direction as the disturbing signal", "Opposite to the disturbing signal", "Only through air", "Only in fibre"], a: 1, e: "Backward crosstalk travels opposite to the aggressor signal.", d: 3 },
    { q: "Why is NEXT usually more harmful than FEXT?", o: ["NEXT only happens in fibre", "At the near end the aggressor is strong while the received signal is already weak", "FEXT is impossible", "NEXT uses higher frequencies"], a: 1, e: "A full-strength disturber meets an attenuated wanted signal at the near end.", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-echo", unit: "u3", title: "Echo, Frequency Shift and Non-Linear Distortion", minutes: 9,
  summary: "Signal reflections (echo) and signal return checks, carrier frequency shift, and non-linear distortion that changes the waveshape.",
  source: "Unit III slides 58–62",
  intro: "<p>Three more impairments change a signal in specific ways: <b>echo</b> sends part of it back, <b>frequency shift</b> moves it to a slightly different frequency, and <b>non-linear distortion</b> bends its shape.</p>",
  definition: "Echo is the reflection of a signal back to its source. Frequency shift is the difference between the transmitted and received frequency, caused by unsynchronised carrier oscillators. Non-linear distortion changes the waveshape because the output is not a linear (scaled) copy of the input, so new frequency components appear.",
  why: "<p>Echo disturbs full-duplex calls and modems, frequency shift corrupts frequency- and phase-based modulation, and non-linear distortion ruins amplitude-modulated carriers. Each has its own remedy.</p>",
  how: "<h3>Echo</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Definition:</b> reflection of a signal back to its source.</li><li><b>Cause:</b> impedance mismatches or reflections in transmission lines.</li><li><b>Impact:</b> degrades quality, especially in full-duplex systems.</li><li><b>Mitigation:</b> echo cancellation — active hybrids and digital signal processing.</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>Signal return</h3><p>A signal sent back from the receiver to the sender, either as an echo or as a response. It is used for:</p><ul class='points'><li><b>Echo checks</b> — received data is sent back to verify integrity.</li><li><b>Error detection</b> — comparing the original with the returned data.</li><li><b>Round-trip time measurement</b> — e.g. the <code>ping</code> command.</li><li><b>Confirming the transmission path.</b></li></ul><p><b>Example:</b> <code>ping</code> uses ICMP echo requests and replies to check connectivity.</p>" +
    "<h3>Frequency shift</h3><p>The difference between the transmitted and received frequency, caused by the <b>lack of synchronisation of the carrier oscillators</b> at the two ends.</p>" +
    "<h3>Non-linear distortion</h3><p>Changes the waveshape of the signal. A sine wave sent and received as a square wave would be severe non-linear distortion. Amplitude-modulated carriers suffer badly when the wave shape is distorted. It happens when the output is not a linearly scaled copy of the input, so <b>new frequency components</b> appear — in amplifiers, loudspeakers and transmission lines.</p>",
  deep: "<div class='codebox'>Reflection coefficient  Γ = (Z_L − Z₀) / (Z_L + Z₀)\nReturn loss (dB)        = −20·log₁₀|Γ|        (higher = less echo)\nEcho delay              = 2 × line length / propagation velocity\n\nExample: 75 Ω line into a 50 Ω load → Γ = −0.2 → 4% of the power reflected, return loss 14 dB</div>" +
    "<p><b>Echo on telephone circuits.</b> The 2-wire/4-wire <b>hybrid</b> never balances perfectly, so some of the talker's own voice returns as <i>talker echo</i>. Short delays sound like a natural sidetone; on long circuits (satellite, VoIP) echo delayed by more than ~25 ms is very annoying. Old networks used <b>echo suppressors</b> (which block the return path, forcing half-duplex). Modern networks and modems use adaptive <b>echo cancellers</b> that subtract an estimate of the echo, keeping true full-duplex.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Frequency shift</b> mattered in analog FDM carrier systems, where single-sideband signals were demodulated with a local oscillator that was not exactly at the original frequency. A shift of a few Hz makes voice sound odd and makes FSK and PSK decisions drift. <i>Doppler shift</i> in mobile and satellite links has a similar effect; receivers track it with phase-locked loops.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Measuring non-linearity.</b> Total harmonic distortion (THD) compares harmonic power with the fundamental. When two signals pass through a non-linear device they also create <i>intermodulation</i> products (2f₁ − f₂, …) that can fall into other FDM channels.</p>",
  examples: ["Hearing your own voice delayed on an international or VoIP call (echo)", "ping 8.8.8.8 measuring round-trip time (signal return)", "A slightly “Donald Duck” voice on old analog carrier systems (frequency shift)", "An overdriven amplifier clipping a sine into a near-square wave (non-linear distortion)"],
  terms: [["Impedance mismatch", "Unequal line and load impedances that cause reflections."], ["Echo canceller", "DSP circuit that subtracts the predicted echo."], ["Hybrid", "2-wire to 4-wire converter; imbalance causes echo."], ["Harmonics", "New frequencies at multiples of the original."], ["Clipping", "Flattening of the peaks when an amplifier saturates."]],
  exam: ["Echo: reflection of a signal back to its source; caused by impedance mismatch; worse in full-duplex; mitigated by echo cancellation.", "Signal return uses: echo checks, error detection, RTT measurement (ping), path confirmation.", "Frequency shift: transmitted vs received frequency difference due to unsynchronised carrier oscillators.", "Non-linear distortion: waveshape changes, new frequency components; AM carriers suffer most."],
  recap: "Echo = reflection (fix: matched impedance, echo cancellers). Frequency shift = oscillators out of sync. Non-linear distortion = shape changes and new frequencies (e.g. clipping).",
  viz: { id: "v-echo", intro: "Three short scenes: a reflection returning from a mismatch, a received wave drifting in frequency, and a sine being clipped." },
  sim: { id: "s-distortion", tasks: ["In Echo mode, set the load to 50 Ω on a 50 Ω line. What is reflected?", "Set the load to 600 Ω. Calculate Γ and check the return loss shown.", "Switch to Non-linear mode and lower the clipping level. Which new harmonics appear?"] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "Echo is:", o: ["A change in waveshape", "The reflection of a signal back to its source", "A frequency difference", "A sudden loss of signal"], a: 1, e: "Echo is a reflected signal returning to the source.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Frequency shift is caused by:", o: ["Impedance mismatch", "Lack of synchronisation of the carrier oscillators", "Lightning", "Crosstalk"], a: 1, e: "Unsynchronised oscillators produce frequency shift.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Which networking command uses echo requests and replies?", o: ["ipconfig", "ping", "nslookup", "netstat"], a: 1, e: "ping sends ICMP echo requests.", d: 2 },
    { q: "A sine wave that arrives as a square wave is an example of:", o: ["Echo", "Non-linear distortion", "Frequency shift", "Attenuation"], a: 1, e: "The waveshape has changed — non-linear distortion.", d: 2 },
    { q: "A 50 Ω line is terminated by a 150 Ω load. The reflection coefficient Γ is:", o: ["0", "0.5", "−0.5", "1"], a: 1, e: "Γ = (150 − 50)/(150 + 50) = 0.5.", d: 3 },
    { q: "Why are echo cancellers preferred over echo suppressors in modern modems?", o: ["They are cheaper wires", "They subtract the echo and keep true full-duplex, whereas suppressors block one direction", "They increase attenuation", "They convert to EBCDIC"], a: 1, e: "Suppressors force half-duplex; cancellers allow full-duplex.", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-jitter", unit: "u3", title: "Jitter: Amplitude and Phase", minutes: 9,
  summary: "What jitter is, its causes and impact on real-time traffic, and the two types — amplitude jitter and phase jitter — with telephone standards.",
  source: "Unit III slides 63–66", related: ["u1-intro", "u3-transients"],
  intro: "<p>Video calls freeze, voices sound robotic — even when the average delay is fine. The culprit is often <b>jitter</b>: delay that keeps changing.</p>",
  definition: "Jitter is the variation in latency over time — the inconsistency of delay, or variability in packet arrival times. On analog lines it appears as small, constantly changing swings in a signal's amplitude (amplitude jitter) or phase (phase jitter).",
  why: "<p>Jitter is measured in milliseconds and directly affects real-time communication. High jitter causes packet loss, poor audio and video quality and streaming interruptions, so it is a key performance objective for real-time networks.</p>",
  how: "<h3>Causes of jitter</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Network congestion</b> — heavy traffic makes packets wait or take longer paths.</li><li><b>Routing changes</b> — packets switch to different paths mid-stream.</li><li><b>Variable path lengths</b> — packets travel different physical distances.</li></ul>" +
    "<h3>Two types of jitter</h3><ul class='points'><li><b>Amplitude jitter</b> — small, constantly changing swings in a signal's amplitude, mainly caused by power-supply noise (60 Hz) and ringing tone (20 Hz) on the signal.</li><li><b>Phase jitter</b> — small, constantly changing swings in a signal's phase. With time-division multiplexing, pulses can move into time slots allocated to other data pulses. Telephone company standards: no more than <b>10°</b> between 20 and 300 Hz, and no more than <b>15°</b> between 4 and 20 Hz.</li></ul>" +
    "<p><b>Jitter amplitude</b> is the peak-to-peak variation in the arrival time of packets or signal transitions, measured in <b>Unit Intervals (UI)</b>. Phase jitter quantifies timing variation in the frequency domain, often calculated by integrating the phase-noise plot over a frequency range. High phase jitter causes reception errors and degraded signal quality.</p>",
  deep: "<p><b>Measuring packet jitter (RFC 3550, used by RTP for voice and video).</b> For consecutive packets i−1 and i, with sending times S and arrival times R:</p>" +
    "<div class='codebox'>D(i−1, i) = (R_i − R_{i−1}) − (S_i − S_{i−1})       ← change in transit time\nJ = J + ( |D(i−1, i)| − J ) / 16                       ← smoothed interarrival jitter\n\nPackets sent every 20 ms arriving at 50, 70, 90, 110 ms → D = 0 → J = 0 (no jitter)\nArriving at 50, 75, 88, 120 ms → D = 5, 7, 12 → J grows</div>" +
    "<p><b>Jitter buffers.</b> VoIP phones hold packets for a short time (e.g. 30–60 ms) and play them out at a steady rate. A bigger buffer absorbs more jitter but adds delay; packets arriving after their playout time count as lost.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Eye diagram.</b> Overlaying many bit periods of a received signal forms an “eye”. Timing jitter closes the eye horizontally, while amplitude noise and jitter close it vertically. The receiver samples at the centre, so a closed eye means bit errors.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Unit interval.</b> 1 UI = one bit period. At 1 Gbit/s, 1 UI = 1 ns, so 0.1 UI of jitter = 100 ps.</p>",
  syllabusNote: "The slides attribute amplitude jitter to power-supply noise at <b>60 Hz</b> — the US mains frequency, since these figures come from US telephone standards. In India the mains frequency is <b>50 Hz</b>, so the hum appears at 50 Hz and its harmonics.",
  examples: ["Robotic or choppy voice on a congested Wi-Fi VoIP call", "A video stream pausing to re-buffer", "Online gaming “lag spikes”", "TDM data errors when pulses drift into a neighbour's slot (phase jitter)"],
  terms: [["Latency", "Time for a packet to travel source → destination."], ["Jitter", "Variation in latency between packets."], ["Unit interval (UI)", "One bit period, used to express timing jitter."], ["Jitter buffer", "Playout buffer that smooths packet arrival."], ["Eye diagram", "Overlay of bit periods showing noise and timing margin."]],
  exam: ["Jitter: variation in latency over time (inconsistency of delay); measured in ms.", "Causes: network congestion, routing changes, variable path lengths.", "Effects: packet loss, poor audio/video, streaming interruptions.", "Amplitude jitter: amplitude swings (power-supply 60 Hz noise, 20 Hz ringing).", "Phase jitter: phase swings; pulses move into other TDM slots; standards ≤10° (20–300 Hz), ≤15° (4–20 Hz).", "Jitter amplitude measured in Unit Intervals (UI)."],
  recap: "Jitter = changing delay. Amplitude jitter wobbles the height; phase jitter wobbles the timing. Fix with jitter buffers, QoS and clean clocks.",
  viz: { id: "v-jitter", intro: "Ideal evenly spaced pulses are compared with pulses suffering amplitude jitter and phase jitter." },
  sim: { id: "s-jitter", tasks: ["Increase timing (phase) jitter and watch the eye diagram close horizontally.", "Increase amplitude jitter and watch it close vertically.", "Read the RFC 3550 jitter value for the packet stream as network congestion increases."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "Jitter is best described as:", o: ["Total delay of a packet", "Variation in latency (delay) over time", "Loss of signal strength", "Reflection of a signal"], a: 1, e: "Jitter is the inconsistency of delay.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Jitter is usually measured in:", o: ["Metres", "Milliseconds", "Volts", "Bytes"], a: 1, e: "Packet jitter is expressed in milliseconds.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Amplitude jitter is principally caused by:", o: ["Lightning only", "Power-supply noise and ringing tone", "Fibre dispersion", "EBCDIC coding"], a: 1, e: "Slides: power-supply noise (60 Hz) and ringing tone (20 Hz).", d: 2 },
    { q: "Which jitter type can push pulses into time slots allocated to other data in TDM?", o: ["Amplitude jitter", "Phase jitter", "Echo", "Crosstalk"], a: 1, e: "Phase jitter shifts timing, so pulses can move into other slots.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Packets sent every 20 ms arrive at 100, 120, 140 and 160 ms. The jitter is:", o: ["20 ms", "100 ms", "0 ms", "160 ms"], a: 2, e: "Arrival spacing equals sending spacing, so delay is constant: zero jitter.", d: 3 },
    { q: "At 1 Gbit/s, a jitter of 0.2 UI corresponds to:", o: ["0.2 ns", "200 ns", "2 ns", "20 ps"], a: 0, e: "1 UI = 1 ns at 1 Gbit/s; 0.2 UI = 0.2 ns = 200 ps.", d: 3 }
  ]
},

{
  id: "u3-transients", unit: "u3", title: "Transients: Impulse Noise, Gain Hits, Dropouts and Phase Hits", minutes: 9,
  summary: "Short-lived disturbances on communication lines — impulse noise, gain hits, dropouts and phase hits — their limits under telephone standards and effects on data.",
  source: "Unit III slides 67–74",
  intro: "<p>Some impairments are not constant but come and go in a flash: a lightning strike, a relay switching, a carrier changing equipment. These brief events are <b>transients</b>, and they cause bursts of errors.</p>",
  definition: "Transients are brief, unwanted electrical signals that disrupt or corrupt data transmission — short-lived, often high-amplitude disturbances caused by lightning, power surges or switching operations. They include impulse noise, gain hits, dropouts and phase hits.",
  why: "<p>Transients cause data errors, equipment damage and dropped connections. Telephone standards limit how often they may occur, and data protocols need error detection and retransmission because of them.</p>",
  how: "<p>Transients may be <b>impulsive</b> (a spike) or <b>oscillatory</b> (ringing), and can be induced into signal or power lines as voltage spikes, current fluctuations or momentary interruptions.</p>" +
    "<h3>Impulse noise</h3><p>Sudden, sharp disturbances of short duration and <b>high amplitude</b>, appearing as isolated pulses within the signal. Causes: electrical interference, voltage spikes, physical disturbances in the medium. Types per the slides:</p><ul class='points'><li><b>High pulse sound</b> — sudden onset, high intensity, short bursts (e.g. gunshots, hammering).</li><li><b>High-energy impulse sound</b> — even greater energy release and intense pressure waves (e.g. rocket launches, explosions, fireworks).</li><li><b>Typical impulse sound</b> — everyday, moderate-intensity bursts (e.g. door slams, horns, alarms).</li></ul><p>It occurs in signal processing, image processing, communications and electronics.</p>" +
    "<h3>Gain hits</h3><p>A sudden <b>increase in amplitude lasting more than 4 ms</b>. Telephone standards allow <b>no more than 8 gain hits in any 15 minutes</b>. On a voice call it sounds as if the volume was turned up for an instant. Amplitude-modulated carriers are particularly sensitive.</p>" +
    "<h3>Dropouts</h3><p>A sudden <b>loss of signal amplitude greater than 12 dB lasting longer than 4 ms</b>. Dropouts cause <b>more errors than any other transient</b>. Standards allow <b>no more than 1 dropout in every 30 minutes</b>. On voice it sounds like call waiting — the line goes dead for about half a second — which is enough for some protocols, such as SLIP, to lose the connection and need to re-establish it.</p>" +
    "<h3>Phase hits</h3><p>Sudden, large changes in the received signal <b>phase (20°) or frequency lasting longer than 4 ms</b>. They generally happen when switching between telcos, common carriers or transmitters. <b>FSK and PSK</b> are particularly sensitive, and the data may be wrong until the phase condition is corrected. Standards allow <b>no more than 8 phase hits in any 15 minutes</b>.</p>",
  compare: { head: ["Transient", "What happens", "Threshold", "Allowed (telephone standards)", "Most affected"], rows: [["Impulse noise", "Short high-amplitude spike", "Brief, isolated pulses", "—", "All data (burst errors)"], ["Gain hit", "Sudden amplitude increase", "> 4 ms", "≤ 8 per 15 min", "AM / ASK, QAM"], ["Dropout", "Sudden amplitude loss", "> 12 dB for > 4 ms", "≤ 1 per 30 min", "All — causes the most errors"], ["Phase hit", "Sudden phase/frequency jump", "> 20° for > 4 ms", "≤ 8 per 15 min", "FSK, PSK"]] },
  deep: "<p><b>Why transients cause burst errors.</b> At 9600 bit/s, a 4 ms dropout wipes out about 38 consecutive bits; at 1 Mbit/s the same event destroys 4000 bits. Simple parity cannot detect many errors in a burst, which is why data links use <b>CRC</b>, which detects all bursts up to its length, plus retransmission.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Matching transients to modulation.</b> Amplitude-based schemes (ASK, QAM) misread bits during <i>gain hits</i>. Phase- and frequency-based schemes (PSK, FSK) misread them during <i>phase hits</i>. Every scheme fails during a <i>dropout</i> because there is no signal at all.</p>" +
    "<p><b>Electrical impulse noise.</b> The slide's examples (gunshots, door slams) describe <i>acoustic</i> impulse noise. On telephone and data lines the equivalent spikes come from lightning, relay and switch operations, motors and power-line faults, so surge protectors and good grounding are the physical defences.</p>",
  examples: ["A crackle or click on a landline during a thunderstorm (impulse noise)", "A momentary volume jump on a call (gain hit)", "A call going silent for half a second (dropout)", "Modem errors when a long-distance carrier switches equipment (phase hit)"],
  terms: [["Transient", "Brief, unwanted disturbance on a line."], ["Burst error", "Many consecutive bits corrupted."], ["SLIP", "Serial Line Internet Protocol; drops on long dropouts."], ["dB drop", "12 dB = signal power falls to about 1/16."]],
  exam: ["Transients: brief unwanted electrical signals (lightning, surges, switching); impulsive or oscillatory.", "Impulse noise: sudden sharp short high-amplitude pulses; types high pulse, high energy, typical impulse.", "Gain hits: sudden amplitude increase > 4 ms; ≤ 8 per 15 min; affects AM carriers.", "Dropouts: amplitude loss > 12 dB for > 4 ms; most errors; ≤ 1 per 30 min; SLIP drops.", "Phase hits: phase change > 20° for > 4 ms; when switching carriers/transmitters; FSK and PSK sensitive; ≤ 8 per 15 min."],
  recap: "Spike (impulse noise), up (gain hit), gone (dropout), jump (phase hit). Dropouts are worst; gain hits hurt ASK/QAM; phase hits hurt FSK/PSK.",
  viz: { id: "v-transients", intro: "A clean carrier is hit in turn by impulse noise, a gain hit, a dropout and a phase hit." },
  sim: { id: "s-transients", tasks: ["Inject a dropout while sending ASK data. How many bits are lost?", "Switch the modulation to PSK and inject a phase hit. Then try a gain hit. Which one breaks PSK?", "Inject 9 gain hits and check the counter against the telephone standard."] },
  quiz: [
    { q: "Transients are:", o: ["Permanent signal loss", "Brief, unwanted electrical disturbances", "A type of modulation", "A character code"], a: 1, e: "Transients are short-lived disturbances.", d: 1 },
    { q: "A sudden increase in amplitude lasting more than 4 ms is a:", o: ["Dropout", "Gain hit", "Phase hit", "Echo"], a: 1, e: "Gain hit = sudden amplitude increase.", d: 1 },
    { q: "Which transient causes more errors than any other type?", o: ["Gain hits", "Phase hits", "Dropouts", "Impulse noise"], a: 2, e: "Dropouts cause the most errors.", d: 2 },
    { q: "Phase hits particularly affect which modulation schemes?", o: ["AM only", "FSK and PSK", "ASCII", "Baseband"], a: 1, e: "FSK and PSK are sensitive to phase/frequency jumps.", d: 2 },
    { q: "According to telephone standards, how many dropouts are allowed?", o: ["8 per 15 minutes", "1 per 30 minutes", "10 per hour", "None"], a: 1, e: "No more than 1 dropout every 30 minutes.", d: 3 },
    { q: "A 4 ms dropout on a 9600 bit/s link destroys approximately how many bits?", o: ["4", "38", "960", "9600"], a: 1, e: "9600 × 0.004 ≈ 38 bits — a burst error.", d: 3 }
  ]
}

